From: HP-UX 11.23 ia64 port notes
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] ipa-devirt: inline the pointer hash to avoid a cross-library call

CANDIDATE / INSUFFICIENT ON ITS OWN -- kept for the record. cc1plus text is
37.6 MB, past the +/-16 MB reach of a direct br.call, and HP ld emits a direct
branch instead of a long-branch stub for htab_hash_pointer. Inlining removes
that one call site, but the linker emits 26 such out-of-range branches, so this
fixes only one of them. The real fix was to link cc1/cc1plus/lto1 with our own
linker.
--- a/gcc/ipa-devirt.c
+++ b/gcc/ipa-devirt.c
@@ -300,6 +300,20 @@
   return (!in_lto_p || odr_type_p (t));
 }
 
+/* PATCH 07 CANDIDATE (ia64-hpux workaround, DIAGNOSTIC): avoid calling libiberty's
+   htab_hash_pointer from here.  HP ld B.12.34 mis-links this particular call in cc1plus: the
+   caller and libiberty end up more than the 16 MB reach of a direct br.call apart (cc1plus text
+   is 37.6 MB), and instead of emitting a long-branch stub the linker emits a direct branch to an
+   address past the end of .text.  Executing it faults with SEGV_MAPERR where si_addr == ip.  It
+   only bites when the ODR type hash table rehashes, which is why it looks input-dependent.
+   htab_hash_pointer is two instructions, so computing it here removes the cross-library call
+   entirely.  Identical result by construction - libiberty's definition is exactly this.  */
+static inline hashval_t
+odr_hash_pointer_inline (const void *p)
+{
+  return (hashval_t) ((intptr_t) p >> 3);
+}
+
 /* Hash type by its ODR name.  */
 
 static hashval_t
@@ -310,11 +324,11 @@
   /* If not in LTO, all main variants are unique, so we can do
      pointer hash.  */
   if (!in_lto_p)
-    return htab_hash_pointer (t);
+    return odr_hash_pointer_inline (t);
 
   /* Anonymous types are unique.  */
   if (type_with_linkage_p (t) && type_in_anonymous_namespace_p (t))
-    return htab_hash_pointer (t);
+    return odr_hash_pointer_inline (t);
 
   gcc_checking_assert (TYPE_NAME (t)
 		       && DECL_ASSEMBLER_NAME_SET_P (TYPE_NAME (t)));
